  "body": "## Finding description and impact\nThe vulnerability originates in the `_updateLimitPostOrder` function where new orders are linked into the order book's doubly-linked list. The critical error occurs when setting the previous order reference:\n```solidity\n// Problematic code in Book.sol\n} else {\n    Order storage tailOrder = self.orders[limit.tailOrder];\n    tailOrder.nextOrderId = order.id;  // Correctly updates storage\n    order.prevOrderId = tailOrder.id;  // ❌ Only updates memory copy\n    limit.tailOrder = order.id;\n}\n```\nThe order parameter is passed as a memory variable, but the function modifies `order.prevOrderId` which only affects the temporary memory copy, not the actual storage. This creates a disconnect in the linked list structure where:\n\n- The previous tail order properly points forward to the new order\n- The new order fails to point backward to the previous tail order in storage\n\n## Impact:\nThis corruption leads to several severe consequences:\n1) #### Order Book Integrity Failure \n - The linked list becomes partially one-directional\n - Traversal from head to tail works, but reverse traversal fails\n - Orders appear orphaned\n2) #### Order Removal Corruption\n```solidity\n// In _updateLimitRemoveOrder\nOrderId prev = order.prevOrderId; // Will be null for affected orders\nOrderId next = order.nextOrderId;\n\nif (!prev.isNull()) self.orders[prev].nextOrderId = next;\nelse limit.headOrder = next; // Will incorrectly execute for orphaned orders\n```\nRemoving an order with missing prev reference causes:\n- Incorrect head order reassignment in the limit price struct\n- Permanent loss of preceding orders\n3) #### Operational Failures\n- Orders become unfillable due to broken links\n- Cancellation attempts may affect wrong orders\n- Price level accounting becomes inaccurate\n- Potential loss of funds as orders get \"stuck\"\n- Corruption propagates as more orders are added/removed\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\nModify _updateLimitPostOrder to properly update storage:\n```solidity\nfunction _updateLimitPostOrder(Book storage self, Limit storage limit, Order memory order) private {\n    limit.numOrders++;\n\n    if (limit.headOrder.isNull()) {\n        limit.headOrder = order.id;\n        limit.tailOrder = order.id;\n    } else {\n        Order storage tailOrder = self.orders[limit.tailOrder];\n        Order storage newOrder = self.orders[order.id]; // Get storage reference\n        \n        tailOrder.nextOrderId = order.id;\n        newOrder.prevOrderId = tailOrder.id; // ✅ Now correctly updates storage\n        limit.tailOrder = order.id;\n        \n        // Add validation\n        require(newOrder.prevOrderId == tailOrder.id, \"Link update failed\");\n    }\n    emit LimitOrderCreated(...);\n}\n```",
  "proof_of_concept": "```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.0;\n\nimport {PoCTestBase} from \"./PoCTestBase.t.sol\";\nimport {Side, Order} from \"contracts/clob/types/Order.sol\";\nimport {ICLOB} from \"contracts/clob/ICLOB.sol\";\nimport {console2} from \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\nimport { Limit} from \"contracts/clob/types/Book.sol\";\nimport {CLOB} from \"contracts/clob/CLOB.sol\";\n\n\n\n\ncontract PoC is PoCTestBase {\n    function test_brokenLimitList() external {\n        address alice = users[0];\n        address bob = users[1];\n        uint256 amountInBase = 2 ether;\n        uint256 price = 100 ether;\n        setupTokens(Side.BUY, alice, amountInBase, price, true);\n        setupTokens(Side.BUY, bob, amountInBase, price, true);\n\n        // Prepare arguments\n        ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs memory args = ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs({\n            amountInBase: amountInBase,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            price: price,\n            cancelTimestamp: NEVER,\n            side: Side.BUY,\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.GOOD_TILL_CANCELLED\n        });\n\n        vm.startPrank(alice);\n        ICLOB.PostLimitOrderResult memory result = clob.postLimitOrder(alice, args);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n        // Verify results\n        assertEq(result.orderId, 1, \"Order ID should be 1\");\n        vm.startPrank(bob);\n        ICLOB.PostLimitOrderResult memory result2 = clob.postLimitOrder(bob, args);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n        assertEq(result2.orderId, 2, \"Order2 ID should be 2\");\n        Order memory order2 = clob.getOrder(result2.orderId);\n        Limit memory limit = clob.getLimit(args.price, args.side);\n        console2.log(\"limit num of orders: \", limit.numOrders);\n        console2.log(\"limit head order id: \", limit.headOrder.unwrap());\n        console2.log(\"limit tail order id: \", limit.tailOrder.unwrap());\n        console2.log(\"Order1 next order id: \", clob.getOrder(result.orderId).nextOrderId.unwrap());\n        console2.log(\"Order2 prev order id: \", order2.prevOrderId.unwrap());\n    }\n\n}\n\n```\ncopy the test contract to `PoC.t.sol` and run with `forge test --match-test test_brokenLimitList -vvv`\n\noutput\n```solidity\n[PASS] test_brokenLimitList() (gas: 691562)\nLogs:\n  limit num of orders:  2\n  limit head order id:  1\n  limit tail order id:  2\n  Order1 next order id:  2\n  Order2 prev order id:  0\n```\n`Order2 prev order id:  0` shows that `order2` is not backward linked to `order1` after creation even though both are in the same limit price struct",
